WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.480
In writing queries, one will sometimes need to&nbsp;
join several tables to get a filter on two fields.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.040
This process can get expensive on resources,&nbsp;
so one alternative is to use a subquery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.560
a SELECT statement inside of a SELECT statement,&nbsp;
and keywords used to meet certain needs. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.000
is one example. I want to show product&nbsp;
names for any product that sold at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:31.200
ten units within a detailed order. Thus,&nbsp;
I have a query set to show the ProductID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:37.200
and the Name from the Production.Product&nbsp;
table, and the criteria I want are located&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.320
in the Sales.SalesOrderDetail table. However, I&nbsp;
don't need to display any fields from that table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.360
which is the key to using a subquery. If you&nbsp;
don't need to display any fields from a table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:53.680
you would otherwise join, a subquery&nbsp;
may be faster. Before I execute this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.800
let's step through this WHERE clause. This&nbsp;
clause states where Product ID equals ANY&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:11.840
ProductID from the Sales.SalesOrderDetail table&nbsp;
where the orderqty &gt;= 10. In executing this, about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:19.280
136 products have had an order quantity of at&nbsp;
least ten. The ANY keyword represents this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.840
If there is at least one match in the subquery,&nbsp;
those records will display in the result set.

